    <!-- ===== Learn screen (docs/FEATURES.md L2) ===== -->

    <!-- Title of the Learn screen. -->
    <string name="learn_title">Learn</string>

    <!-- Spoken description of the back arrow in the top app bar. -->
    <string name="learn_back">Back</string>

    <!-- Section heading: what the calendar is. -->
    <string name="learn_section_what">What is the International Fixed Calendar?</string>

    <!-- First "what is it" paragraph: the month/day structure. -->
    <string name="learn_what_months">The International Fixed Calendar (IFC) reshapes the year into 13 months of exactly 28 days each — four whole weeks, every month, every year, forever.</string>

    <!-- Second "what is it" paragraph: Sol. -->
    <string name="learn_what_sol">The extra, 13th month is called Sol, and it sits between June and July.</string>

    <!-- Third "what is it" paragraph: every month starts Sunday, and the Friday-the-13th identity. -->
    <string name="learn_what_friday_13">Because every month is exactly 4 weeks, every month starts on a nominal Sunday and ends on a nominal Saturday — and the 13th of every month nominally falls on a Friday.</string>

    <!-- Section heading: the two days that belong to no week. -->
    <string name="learn_section_floating">The two days outside the week</string>

    <!-- Intro paragraph explaining why the floating days exist. -->
    <string name="learn_floating_intro">13 months of 28 days add up to 364 days — one short of a full year, two short in a leap year. Those extra days do not belong to any week or month; the IFC sets them apart as Year Day and Leap Day.</string>

    <!-- Year Day paragraph. %1$s = its IFC long form, e.g. "Year Day, 2026". %2$s = its Gregorian long form. -->
    <string name="learn_floating_year_day">Year Day happens every year. It comes after December 28 and before next January 1 — it belongs to no week and has no IFC weekday of its own. %1$s is Gregorian %2$s.</string>

    <!-- Leap Day paragraph. %1$s = its IFC long form, e.g. "Leap Day, 2024". %2$s = its Gregorian long form. -->
    <string name="learn_floating_leap_day">Leap Day happens only in leap years. It comes after June 28 and before Sol 1 — also outside the week. %1$s is Gregorian %2$s.</string>

    <!-- Section heading: nominal vs. actual weekday. -->
    <string name="learn_section_weekday">Why the weekdays in this app differ</string>

    <!-- Intro paragraph: how the IFC gets a perpetual weekday, and why the real world does not follow it. -->
    <string name="learn_weekday_intro">The IFC gets its perpetual weekdays by taking Year Day and Leap Day out of the week entirely. The real world does not do this: the seven-day cycle runs on, unbroken, through December 31 and June 17 every year.</string>

    <!-- The spec's own worked example. %1$s = Gregorian long form with weekday. %2$s = IFC long form.
         %3$s = the IFC (nominal) weekday name. %4$s = the real (actual) weekday name. -->
    <string name="learn_weekday_worked_example">For example: %1$s is IFC %2$s. Its IFC weekday is %3$s, but because Year Day (and, in leap years, Leap Day) are taken out of the count first, its real weekday is %4$s.</string>

    <!-- Conclusion: what the app does about it. -->
    <string name="learn_weekday_conclusion">This is why the app always shows both weekdays: the IFC one for the calendar\'s own sense of the week, and the real one for anything tied to actual life — today\'s highlight, your events, and your reminders.</string>

    <!-- Section heading: how dates are calculated. -->
    <string name="learn_section_calculation">How the dates are calculated</string>

    <!-- Day-of-year equality and the canonical numeric form. %1$s = the numeric IFC form with its
         mandatory "IFC" marker, e.g. "IFC 2026-10-08". -->
    <string name="learn_calc_day_of_year">The IFC\'s day-of-year number is always identical to the Gregorian one. The calendar\'s own numeric date form always carries an \"IFC\" marker, like %1$s, so it can never be mistaken for an ordinary Gregorian date written the same way.</string>

    <!-- Sol 1 is always June 18. %1$s/%3$s = IFC long forms for a common and a leap year.
         %2$s/%4$s = their Gregorian long forms. -->
    <string name="learn_calc_sol1">Sol 1 is always Gregorian June 18, whether the year is a leap year or not: %1$s is Gregorian %2$s, and %3$s is Gregorian %4$s.</string>

    <!-- Year Day is always December 31. %1$s = IFC long form. %2$s = Gregorian long form. -->
    <string name="learn_calc_year_day">Year Day is always Gregorian December 31: %1$s is Gregorian %2$s.</string>

    <!-- The leap-year shift for March through June. %1$s = IFC long form in the common-year example.
         %2$d = that year. %3$s = IFC long form in the leap-year example. %4$d = that year. -->
    <string name="learn_calc_leap_shift">In a leap year, Leap Day is inserted after June 28, which pushes every IFC date from March through June one day later than in a common year. For example, Gregorian March 1 is %1$s in a common year like %2$d, but %3$s in a leap year like %4$d.</string>

    <!-- The Friday-the-13th identity, worked out. %1$s = IFC long form, e.g. "January 13, 2026".
         %2$s = the nominal weekday name, e.g. "Friday". -->
    <string name="learn_calc_friday_13">And because every month is exactly 4 weeks, the 13th of every month is always a nominal Friday: %1$s\'s IFC weekday is %2$s.</string>

    <!-- Section heading: history. -->
    <string name="learn_section_history">A brief history</string>

    <!-- Cotsworth. The book's date is given as a range because sources disagree (calendar-spec §2.5). -->
    <string name="learn_history_cotsworth">Moses B. Cotsworth (1859–1943), a British railway statistician, devised the plan so that monthly figures could be compared fairly, and presented it in 1902; his book explaining it, \"The Rational Almanac,\" is dated 1902 or 1905 depending on the source.</string>

    <!-- League of Nations. -->
    <string name="learn_history_league_of_nations">The League of Nations took up calendar reform in the 1920s. According to Wikipedia, its committee judged the 13-month plan the best of 130 proposals and held an international conference on it in October 1931 — but it was never adopted, largely because days outside the week broke the continuous seven-day week observed by several religions.</string>

    <!-- Eastman Kodak. The end date is given loosely because sources disagree (calendar-spec §2.5: most
         say 1989, one says 1982). -->
    <string name="learn_history_kodak">George Eastman became the plan\'s main backer in the mid-1920s and put it to work at Eastman Kodak starting in 1928; Kodak\'s 13-period calendar stayed in internal use for decades — until around 1989, by most accounts.</string>

    <!-- Closing historical fact. -->
    <string name="learn_history_never_adopted">No country has ever adopted the International Fixed Calendar.</string>

    <!-- Section heading: FAQ. -->
    <string name="learn_section_faq">Frequently asked questions</string>

    <!-- FAQ: is it lunar? -->
    <string name="learn_faq_lunar_question">Is the IFC based on the phases of the moon?</string>
    <string name="learn_faq_lunar_answer">No. Despite having 13 months, the IFC is a solar calendar: its year is exactly as long as the Gregorian year, and its months are fixed at 28 days regardless of the moon\'s roughly 29.5-day cycle.</string>

    <!-- FAQ: does the year start in spring? -->
    <string name="learn_faq_year_start_question">Does the IFC year start in spring?</string>
    <string name="learn_faq_year_start_answer">No. IFC January 1 is Gregorian January 1, the same as every other year — the IFC relabels the days inside the existing Gregorian year rather than starting the year somewhere else.</string>

    <!-- FAQ: Sol vs. Leap Day. -->
    <string name="learn_faq_sol_vs_leap_question">Are Sol and Leap Day the same thing?</string>
    <string name="learn_faq_sol_vs_leap_answer">No, they\'re different kinds of thing. Sol is a full, 28-day month between June and July, present every year. Leap Day is a single day with no week and no month, present only in leap years, coming after June 28.</string>

    <!-- FAQ: other 13-month calendars. -->
    <string name="learn_faq_other_calendars_question">Is this the only 13-month calendar?</string>
    <string name="learn_faq_other_calendars_answer">No. Other 13-month proposals exist — among them the Positivist calendar, the World Calendar (which actually has 12 months) and the Pax calendar — and each places its extra days differently, or keeps the seven-day week intact instead. If a 13-month calendar has a month called Sol between June and July, a Year Day on Gregorian December 31 and a Leap Day on Gregorian June 17, it is this one, the IFC; otherwise, it\'s a different calendar.</string>

    <!-- FAQ: what "proleptic" means in the converter. -->
    <string name="learn_faq_proleptic_question">What does the converter\'s note about \"proleptic\" dates mean?</string>
    <string name="learn_faq_proleptic_answer">For any date before a country actually adopted the Gregorian calendar, the app still uses today\'s calendar rules extended backward in time — historians call this \"proleptic.\" It lets the converter answer for any year from 1 to 9999, but a date from, say, the 1600s is a hypothetical translation, not a date anyone alive then would have recognized.</string>

    <!-- Spoken state of an expanded FAQ answer, for TalkBack. -->
    <string name="learn_faq_state_expanded">Expanded</string>

    <!-- Spoken state of a collapsed FAQ answer, for TalkBack. -->
    <string name="learn_faq_state_collapsed">Collapsed</string>

